WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:07.500
&gt;&gt; The speakers that I did
not introduce in the morning,

00:00:07.501 --> 00:00:08.945
I don't think he also
needs introduction

00:00:08.946 --> 00:00:10.560
as Professor Raj
Reddy mentioned,

00:00:10.561 --> 00:00:11.785
does binding work in

00:00:11.786 --> 00:00:13.585
Computer Vision Machine
Learning et cetera,

00:00:13.586 --> 00:00:15.480
so I'll let Professor Jawahar
take the stage. Thanks.

00:00:15.481 --> 00:00:17.485
&gt;&gt; Thank you for
the opportunity.

00:00:17.486 --> 00:00:20.620
So I'll try to give

00:00:20.621 --> 00:00:25.780
a quick overview of
what is happening in

00:00:25.781 --> 00:00:28.750
the computer vision area
in recent past

00:00:28.751 --> 00:00:34.925
and hinting at the major
trends in this space.

00:00:34.926 --> 00:00:37.210
However, when I tried
to summarize this,

00:00:37.211 --> 00:00:40.025
I found that it is
not very easy to

00:00:40.026 --> 00:00:43.380
compile or put together
the major themes in this space,

00:00:43.381 --> 00:00:44.940
so I might have missed a

00:00:44.941 --> 00:00:46.295
lot of things that
are happening,

00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:48.940
but let me give you an attempt.

00:00:48.941 --> 00:00:51.650
So Computer Vision as a task,

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:53.885
like as an image
understanding task,

00:00:53.886 --> 00:00:58.615
has been evolving over
many years, decades,

00:00:58.616 --> 00:01:01.010
and the focus has been

00:01:01.011 --> 00:01:04.340
on understanding
the content of it,

00:01:04.341 --> 00:01:07.005
in terms of what is a scene,

00:01:07.006 --> 00:01:08.815
what are the objects presented,

00:01:08.816 --> 00:01:10.315
where are the objects presented,

00:01:10.316 --> 00:01:11.940
how are they placed.

00:01:11.941 --> 00:01:13.445
Who is close to what,

00:01:13.446 --> 00:01:16.165
a set of questions that
you can infer out of it,

00:01:16.166 --> 00:01:18.980
so this has been the broad focus

00:01:18.981 --> 00:01:21.380
of Computer Vision
for many years,

00:01:21.381 --> 00:01:23.510
or at least a dream of
Computer Vision researchers

00:01:23.511 --> 00:01:26.900
and practitioners
for many years.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:31.680
So now, to give you

00:01:31.681 --> 00:01:34.795
the background of how
this community has evolved,

00:01:34.796 --> 00:01:36.830
the concern has always

00:01:36.831 --> 00:01:39.595
like given an image
how to interpret.

00:01:39.596 --> 00:01:42.290
What is in it? How do I

00:01:42.291 --> 00:01:45.705
know whether my understanding
of the image is good?

00:01:45.706 --> 00:01:48.850
Whether my processing results
of the image is good,

00:01:48.851 --> 00:01:50.745
so that we are happy with

00:01:50.746 --> 00:01:52.740
the success or failure
of that algorithm.

00:01:52.741 --> 00:01:55.500
At least we know if it
is succeeding or not.

00:01:55.501 --> 00:02:00.150
So we'll take
a history lesson from

00:02:00.151 --> 00:02:02.420
the low-level vision
or mid-level vision

00:02:02.421 --> 00:02:04.070
where anybody who has ever tried

00:02:04.071 --> 00:02:05.760
to understand the process image,

00:02:05.761 --> 00:02:07.225
one of the first thing
that we all learn

00:02:07.226 --> 00:02:09.410
to do is to detect edges,

00:02:09.411 --> 00:02:12.005
detect with
the Sobel operators or

00:02:12.006 --> 00:02:14.420
the small tiny mass that we all

00:02:14.421 --> 00:02:17.400
learn to write programs
to detect edges.

00:02:17.401 --> 00:02:21.470
How, if you look at
an image like this,

00:02:21.471 --> 00:02:23.000
an natural image like this,

00:02:23.001 --> 00:02:26.030
where if you want to
detect an edge or a contour

00:02:26.031 --> 00:02:30.220
or separate an object
the task is very difficult.

00:02:30.221 --> 00:02:33.340
And very often, very subjective.

00:02:33.341 --> 00:02:35.780
So one of the first attempt in

00:02:35.781 --> 00:02:38.000
this space has
been to understand

00:02:38.001 --> 00:02:39.855
the subjectivity by the help of

00:02:39.856 --> 00:02:43.690
multiple human experts
saying what's an object,

00:02:43.691 --> 00:02:45.760
what's an edge,
what's a contour,

00:02:45.761 --> 00:02:48.250
and then using this
as a benchmark,

00:02:48.251 --> 00:02:50.645
trying to see how
algorithms perform,

00:02:50.646 --> 00:02:52.410
and how to characterize
the algorithm

00:02:52.411 --> 00:02:53.670
with the performance metric

00:02:53.671 --> 00:02:57.035
which we can compare and
see the progress over years.

00:02:57.036 --> 00:02:59.940
And we see that, over years,

00:02:59.941 --> 00:03:02.455
we had approached
closer and closer to

00:03:02.456 --> 00:03:07.360
human limits of understanding
the object contour,

00:03:07.361 --> 00:03:10.500
and we are now

00:03:10.501 --> 00:03:14.200
reasonably good at processing
some of these tasks.

00:03:14.201 --> 00:03:16.450
But the modern Computer
Vision said there's

00:03:16.451 --> 00:03:19.415
mostly on a higher level task.

00:03:19.416 --> 00:03:21.960
So in general, this sort of

00:03:21.961 --> 00:03:24.030
an attempt, gives
us few lessons.

00:03:24.031 --> 00:03:27.995
One, we need a formal
definition of the task,

00:03:27.996 --> 00:03:30.970
and a realistic dataset
with annotation on

00:03:30.971 --> 00:03:34.230
which we can evaluate
the performance,

00:03:34.231 --> 00:03:38.390
and measure that can quantify
the performance basically.

00:03:38.391 --> 00:03:44.390
Algorithms that can use
this annotated data.

00:03:44.391 --> 00:03:46.705
And on top of it,

00:03:46.706 --> 00:03:48.480
how a community
can use this data

00:03:48.481 --> 00:03:52.610
and see the progress over years.

00:03:52.611 --> 00:03:57.640
So, this has been
the primary driving force behind

00:03:57.641 --> 00:03:59.550
the Computer Vision research in

00:03:59.551 --> 00:04:04.115
the last couple of
decades seriously.

00:04:04.116 --> 00:04:06.340
So in the higher level task,

00:04:06.341 --> 00:04:08.090
where the task is usually not

00:04:08.091 --> 00:04:10.100
just detecting the extent of

00:04:10.101 --> 00:04:12.180
the objects or where
they are present or

00:04:12.181 --> 00:04:15.220
understanding the properties
of the pixels or regions,

00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:17.235
it has been mostly recognition.

00:04:17.236 --> 00:04:19.410
Really, attempts on
recognition was on

00:04:19.411 --> 00:04:21.580
objects which have
lot of presence in

00:04:21.581 --> 00:04:25.950
natural images like
humans, human faces, text,

00:04:25.951 --> 00:04:30.255
characters, character
recognitions and vehicles,

00:04:30.256 --> 00:04:32.750
and understanding their poses or

00:04:32.751 --> 00:04:36.250
positions in natural images.

00:04:36.251 --> 00:04:39.640
So the history of
the modern Computer Vision or at

00:04:39.641 --> 00:04:44.525
least which became
very hot in post 2000

00:04:44.526 --> 00:04:50.055
where the task of understanding
were formally casted on

00:04:50.056 --> 00:04:52.165
a wide variety of natural images

00:04:52.166 --> 00:04:55.755
internet repositories of
images became very popular.

00:04:55.756 --> 00:04:57.730
We all had our own cameras,

00:04:57.731 --> 00:05:00.010
and we all had captured and we

00:05:00.011 --> 00:05:02.180
found that the applications
of Computer Vision will

00:05:02.181 --> 00:05:04.700
have to be on natural data

00:05:04.701 --> 00:05:07.285
captured in the wild conditions.

00:05:07.286 --> 00:05:09.795
So in these stage,

00:05:09.796 --> 00:05:12.390
the communities started building

00:05:12.391 --> 00:05:14.935
datasets and task navigations.

00:05:14.936 --> 00:05:16.835
So the initial attempt of

00:05:16.836 --> 00:05:19.030
object recognition was captured,

00:05:19.031 --> 00:05:20.515
natural object is captured,

00:05:20.516 --> 00:05:22.445
images of the objects,

00:05:22.446 --> 00:05:24.780
it may be in a focused manner.

00:05:24.781 --> 00:05:28.105
And then, when it came
to Pascal animates that,

00:05:28.106 --> 00:05:31.690
the late 2005 and onwards.

00:05:31.691 --> 00:05:33.680
We were capturing images or

00:05:33.681 --> 00:05:35.520
processing images
which are more and more

00:05:35.521 --> 00:05:39.210
wild than naturally captured
in their natural settings.

00:05:39.211 --> 00:05:40.770
And the past became more,

00:05:40.771 --> 00:05:43.500
and also vary more and
more formally defined,

00:05:43.501 --> 00:05:46.665
initially the task was to
say, what is this object?

00:05:46.666 --> 00:05:49.680
Then it became like tell
me where is this object in

00:05:49.681 --> 00:05:53.635
this image and how is it placed?

00:05:53.636 --> 00:05:55.510
Third level, we
went to the level

00:05:55.511 --> 00:05:59.175
of tell me which pixels
are on this image itself,

00:05:59.176 --> 00:06:02.550
so the task became, even

00:06:02.551 --> 00:06:04.685
if it's a bike,
which bike it is.

00:06:04.686 --> 00:06:06.545
The fine-grained
understanding of

00:06:06.546 --> 00:06:11.280
the visual content in
terms of the pixels,

00:06:11.281 --> 00:06:13.885
were all, in terms
of the properties,

00:06:13.886 --> 00:06:16.715
like which bike or
which make of is it,

00:06:16.716 --> 00:06:19.945
this level of task got formally

00:06:19.946 --> 00:06:22.790
stated and the
appropriate datasets

00:06:22.791 --> 00:06:24.990
for this task came
into the literature.

00:06:24.991 --> 00:06:30.385
And this was the
major phase where the,

00:06:30.386 --> 00:06:32.290
the formal definitions and

00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:34.685
improvements in
the community was measured.

00:06:34.686 --> 00:06:35.845
The Pascal, for example,

00:06:35.846 --> 00:06:38.600
which started in 2005; by 2012,

00:06:38.601 --> 00:06:40.620
they found that, okay,
this task will have to move

00:06:40.621 --> 00:06:43.480
forward with a new set of tasks.

00:06:43.481 --> 00:06:44.770
The industry at the same time

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:48.385
the natural datasets
collected with the ImageNet,

00:06:48.386 --> 00:06:52.300
one of the largest datasets
available of images which

00:06:52.301 --> 00:06:57.435
had extremely large number
of categories of images,

00:06:57.436 --> 00:06:59.750
and the number of examples,

00:06:59.751 --> 00:07:02.580
became also the one of

00:07:02.581 --> 00:07:04.290
the very important player

00:07:04.291 --> 00:07:06.445
in the emergence of
the Deep Learning,

00:07:06.446 --> 00:07:14.440
because the turning point

00:07:14.441 --> 00:07:17.605
in this history has been
this AlexNet which appeared in

00:07:17.606 --> 00:07:21.280
2012 where the task on image,

00:07:21.281 --> 00:07:23.100
the classification,
the performance

00:07:23.101 --> 00:07:24.470
of the previous era like

00:07:24.471 --> 00:07:26.050
the last five years we have been

00:07:26.051 --> 00:07:28.790
engaging by like improvements
by one percentage,

00:07:28.791 --> 00:07:31.115
half percentage over years.

00:07:31.116 --> 00:07:35.720
While in 2012, a completely
different approach which is

00:07:35.721 --> 00:07:37.610
an end-to-end trading off

00:07:37.611 --> 00:07:39.660
a deep neural network give

00:07:39.661 --> 00:07:43.150
a significant improvement
of close to 10 percentage.

00:07:43.151 --> 00:07:47.890
This attracted the
researchers, and this made,

00:07:47.891 --> 00:07:50.730
this convinced
the larger wider community

00:07:50.731 --> 00:07:52.390
that Deep Learning

00:07:52.391 --> 00:07:57.140
is robust and this is usable
for a variety of tasks,

00:07:57.141 --> 00:08:03.475
and people can get into this
and the rest is possibly,

00:08:03.476 --> 00:08:06.375
still modern Computer Vision.

00:08:06.376 --> 00:08:14.520
So, if you look at the
historical developments of SIFT,

00:08:14.521 --> 00:08:16.930
and the previous
Deep Learning era,

00:08:16.931 --> 00:08:18.545
the performance was 25.

00:08:18.546 --> 00:08:20.605
And from 2012 onwards,

00:08:20.606 --> 00:08:22.715
like very very very fast,

00:08:22.716 --> 00:08:26.545
we came down in
the error rates and became

00:08:26.546 --> 00:08:28.940
quite comparable to
the human error rates

00:08:28.941 --> 00:08:31.720
in say, three, four years time.

00:08:31.721 --> 00:08:35.870
A number of deep,
deeper and deeper architectures

00:08:35.871 --> 00:08:39.525
came with better and
better learning algorithms,

00:08:39.526 --> 00:08:41.720
better and better methods

00:08:41.721 --> 00:08:44.180
to design architectures
which are more compact,

00:08:44.181 --> 00:08:46.480
efficient, and this is

00:08:46.481 --> 00:08:52.880
where the post Deep Learning
era, the architectures mode.

00:08:52.881 --> 00:08:57.620
Now, from a pure Computer
Vision point of view,

00:08:57.621 --> 00:09:01.320
there are lot of
other concerns in terms of,

00:09:01.321 --> 00:09:04.965
what do we mean by
understanding images?

00:09:04.966 --> 00:09:07.710
And how do we
represent the images?

00:09:07.711 --> 00:09:10.070
The features or
the representation

00:09:10.071 --> 00:09:11.470
of the image has been very,

00:09:11.471 --> 00:09:12.770
very fundamental issue in

00:09:12.771 --> 00:09:16.285
the image understanding
or image analysis space.

00:09:16.286 --> 00:09:19.410
So if you would like
to have features or

00:09:19.411 --> 00:09:22.500
representations
which are superior,

00:09:22.501 --> 00:09:25.285
and the previous era,
previous to deep learning,

00:09:25.286 --> 00:09:28.125
we have been doing
mostly histogram-based features,

00:09:28.126 --> 00:09:30.875
borrowing ideas
from bag-of-words,

00:09:30.876 --> 00:09:33.440
and a number of variations
of it which connects

00:09:33.441 --> 00:09:38.280
to the IR community, signal
processing community.

00:09:38.281 --> 00:09:43.145
Now, since the image
classification problem

00:09:43.146 --> 00:09:46.420
is solved for
a specific ImageNet task,

00:09:46.421 --> 00:09:48.955
we ask the natural question
that, "Okay look,

00:09:48.956 --> 00:09:52.195
what does it mean to the
larger Computer Vision space?"

00:09:52.196 --> 00:09:54.850
So, the answer is,
the features that we

00:09:54.851 --> 00:10:00.415
compute by trading
the ImageNet are generic.

00:10:00.416 --> 00:10:02.910
This give us a new hope that,

00:10:02.911 --> 00:10:05.890
a network which is trained
to do a certain task in

00:10:05.891 --> 00:10:07.600
the image space is fully

00:10:07.601 --> 00:10:10.385
useful for a number
of other tasks.

00:10:10.386 --> 00:10:12.810
You train the neural network or

00:10:12.811 --> 00:10:15.580
deep learning algorithm
for a separate task.

00:10:15.581 --> 00:10:20.110
However, and their
process you build a set of

00:10:20.111 --> 00:10:22.220
representations which
are widely useful

00:10:22.221 --> 00:10:26.070
for a wide variety of
other Computer Vision tasks.

00:10:26.071 --> 00:10:28.835
So you have
a neural network here.

00:10:28.836 --> 00:10:30.900
We just trained on
the specific task

00:10:30.901 --> 00:10:32.265
of classification, however,

00:10:32.266 --> 00:10:35.330
if you stop at some layer and
make a representation that

00:10:35.331 --> 00:10:38.430
is useful for
recognizing a new object,

00:10:38.431 --> 00:10:41.890
or a new class, or
a new type of visual data.

00:10:41.891 --> 00:10:43.640
Now, you can reuse
this and you can

00:10:43.641 --> 00:10:45.775
also do refine these features.

00:10:45.776 --> 00:10:47.605
You can improve this features

00:10:47.606 --> 00:10:49.880
with the help of
transfer learning,

00:10:49.881 --> 00:10:52.250
or you can adapt
the knowledge that is trained

00:10:52.251 --> 00:10:54.950
in one domain of the one domain,

00:10:54.951 --> 00:10:56.230
or one category of images,

00:10:56.231 --> 00:10:57.620
or one type of images,

00:10:57.621 --> 00:11:00.490
to a new type with
relative ease.

00:11:00.491 --> 00:11:02.200
And people in fact have looked

00:11:02.201 --> 00:11:04.550
into how generic or
critical is this.

00:11:04.551 --> 00:11:08.385
The amount of data
into the first domain.

00:11:08.386 --> 00:11:10.395
It is found that,

00:11:10.396 --> 00:11:12.650
with a small amount of
pre-training itself,

00:11:12.651 --> 00:11:15.040
you can build
solutions which can

00:11:15.041 --> 00:11:17.980
be very useful for a new task.

00:11:17.981 --> 00:11:20.580
So, in this case,

00:11:20.581 --> 00:11:23.370
a Deep Neural Network
which was trained

00:11:23.371 --> 00:11:27.510
on ImageNet is completely

00:11:27.511 --> 00:11:30.600
acceptable to a new task
of same classification,

00:11:30.601 --> 00:11:32.620
which is completely
a new set of images,

00:11:32.621 --> 00:11:35.835
new task definition itself.

00:11:35.836 --> 00:11:40.100
And naturally, the concern or

00:11:40.101 --> 00:11:44.900
the positive result of
seeing a generic features,

00:11:44.901 --> 00:11:46.980
also resulted in curiosity

00:11:46.981 --> 00:11:49.275
of what these neural
networks learn,

00:11:49.276 --> 00:11:55.155
and found that
the learned concepts are- If

00:11:55.156 --> 00:11:58.610
you try to open the deep
neural network and look

00:11:58.611 --> 00:12:02.630
at what neural network
learns over these layers.

00:12:02.631 --> 00:12:05.655
It is interesting to see
that the initial layers of

00:12:05.656 --> 00:12:06.970
these deep neural networks are

00:12:06.971 --> 00:12:09.355
learning features,
which are very generic.

00:12:09.356 --> 00:12:12.955
Like small edges or corners,

00:12:12.956 --> 00:12:14.240
and the later layers were

00:12:14.241 --> 00:12:16.705
learning semantically
richer concepts,

00:12:16.706 --> 00:12:19.020
like objects or object parts.

00:12:19.021 --> 00:12:21.655
And this gave us

00:12:21.656 --> 00:12:24.985
explanation of what how
these features are generic,

00:12:24.986 --> 00:12:26.250
and how do you transfer

00:12:26.251 --> 00:12:29.870
these features from one space
to the another space.

00:12:29.871 --> 00:12:32.190
Then came the question
that, "Okay look,

00:12:32.191 --> 00:12:33.815
we have been seeing
only the success

00:12:33.816 --> 00:12:35.410
of supervised learning.

00:12:35.411 --> 00:12:38.175
Can we do something
beyond that?"

00:12:38.176 --> 00:12:41.150
Creating annotated data for

00:12:41.151 --> 00:12:45.245
various task is tedious.
And how do we avoid it?

00:12:45.246 --> 00:12:48.650
So the vision community
had the answer to this was,

00:12:48.651 --> 00:12:50.380
use the larger context,

00:12:50.381 --> 00:12:53.530
and learn in this way
so that later

00:12:53.531 --> 00:12:56.050
on or the features that

00:12:56.051 --> 00:12:58.950
you learn in this process
is useful for other tasks.

00:12:58.951 --> 00:13:02.135
To give an example,
if you have an image,

00:13:02.136 --> 00:13:03.630
and you want to guess,

00:13:03.631 --> 00:13:05.370
you chop or you mask

00:13:05.371 --> 00:13:07.730
a certain region of the image
and say, what it will be.

00:13:07.731 --> 00:13:10.225
So similar to the ideas
from Word2vec.

00:13:10.226 --> 00:13:12.960
Where you are given
a sentence and remove

00:13:12.961 --> 00:13:15.360
word and ask you to predict
the word given the context.

00:13:15.361 --> 00:13:17.350
Similarly, given
the rest of the image,

00:13:17.351 --> 00:13:20.215
can you predict what could
be there in this patch?

00:13:20.216 --> 00:13:22.910
And if you're able
to do this task,

00:13:22.911 --> 00:13:27.000
then the features that you
are learning, is also generic.

00:13:27.001 --> 00:13:32.495
So this sort of ideas of
learning from surrounding text,

00:13:32.496 --> 00:13:35.815
learning from previous frames,

00:13:35.816 --> 00:13:39.040
learning from parallel
data, for example,

00:13:39.041 --> 00:13:40.525
the case of Wikipedia pages,

00:13:40.526 --> 00:13:42.470
like Wikipedia images and text,

00:13:42.471 --> 00:13:45.550
so the text topic models
can act as learning

00:13:45.551 --> 00:13:47.345
signal or supervisory signal

00:13:47.346 --> 00:13:49.495
to the image
classification task.

00:13:49.496 --> 00:13:51.960
And so, this entire class
of algorithms which

00:13:51.961 --> 00:13:55.280
does not really use
the fully supervised learning,

00:13:55.281 --> 00:13:57.390
which use innovate unsupervised

00:13:57.391 --> 00:13:58.765
or self-supervised learning,

00:13:58.766 --> 00:14:00.205
or learning from itself

00:14:00.206 --> 00:14:04.000
by harvesting
the supervisory signal

00:14:04.001 --> 00:14:07.045
within it became very popular.

00:14:07.046 --> 00:14:10.440
Now, we had seen
the features that

00:14:10.441 --> 00:14:13.180
are useful for missions
for processing.

00:14:13.181 --> 00:14:15.890
What about humans?

00:14:15.891 --> 00:14:19.735
If you want to have representations
that are more useful,

00:14:19.736 --> 00:14:21.900
one way to look at this is,

00:14:21.901 --> 00:14:24.700
building representations
with are human

00:14:24.701 --> 00:14:26.475
understandable and which human

00:14:26.476 --> 00:14:28.145
should be able to manipulate.

00:14:28.146 --> 00:14:32.930
So this was attempted with
the help of by connecting to

00:14:32.931 --> 00:14:35.090
language and in this era
that there is lot

00:14:35.091 --> 00:14:37.895
of connection between
vision and language first.

00:14:37.896 --> 00:14:39.570
So, instead of understanding

00:14:39.571 --> 00:14:41.580
the image as a classification,

00:14:41.581 --> 00:14:44.845
the newer tasks like captioning.

00:14:44.846 --> 00:14:47.360
Given an image can you
write a sentence about it?

00:14:47.361 --> 00:14:50.510
Or given an image can you
write a number of phrases

00:14:50.511 --> 00:14:53.895
corresponding to what this cat
is doing in this image?

00:14:53.896 --> 00:14:56.415
Or the relationship
between objects?

00:14:56.416 --> 00:14:58.800
Like cat sits on something.

00:14:58.801 --> 00:15:04.585
So this level of phrases or
the corrections are first.

00:15:04.586 --> 00:15:07.260
So, in general, just looked

00:15:07.261 --> 00:15:10.290
at a set of supervised methods,

00:15:10.291 --> 00:15:13.735
where you have an image
and a set of sentences,

00:15:13.736 --> 00:15:16.570
and you'd like to learn
the relationship between them,

00:15:16.571 --> 00:15:19.950
and learn models so that you
can generate a number of,

00:15:19.951 --> 00:15:24.205
a caption or human
understandable description

00:15:24.206 --> 00:15:26.130
by composing the understanding

00:15:26.131 --> 00:15:28.795
of the objects and
their relationships.

00:15:28.796 --> 00:15:31.135
So, these are few more examples,

00:15:31.136 --> 00:15:33.815
where we are able to generate

00:15:33.816 --> 00:15:40.380
the human-like captions for
the corresponding images.

00:15:40.381 --> 00:15:43.120
So, here you can see a number of

00:15:43.121 --> 00:15:47.305
such and the compositional
descriptions appearing here,

00:15:47.306 --> 00:15:50.180
where objects and
their surroundings

00:15:50.181 --> 00:15:52.710
are connected together
to come up with phrases,

00:15:52.711 --> 00:15:57.415
or captions, and different
resolutions in the space.

00:15:57.416 --> 00:16:02.945
So this space has been
flooded with atoms in 2015,

00:16:02.946 --> 00:16:06.465
'16's time, with a number
of architectures,

00:16:06.466 --> 00:16:08.370
which used convolutional neural

00:16:08.371 --> 00:16:11.900
networks and recurrent
neural networks.

00:16:11.901 --> 00:16:15.700
Beyond that, then
the next question is,

00:16:15.701 --> 00:16:17.220
like if you have such images,

00:16:17.221 --> 00:16:19.025
can you interact
with such images?

00:16:19.026 --> 00:16:21.465
Can you do the dialogs
based on these images?

00:16:21.466 --> 00:16:24.585
Can you understand what
is there in this image,

00:16:24.586 --> 00:16:27.240
and can you ask relevant
questions and get answers?

00:16:27.241 --> 00:16:29.075
Can you have continuous
dialog with them?

00:16:29.076 --> 00:16:32.980
A number of open task
in the space came out.

00:16:32.981 --> 00:16:34.655
And what is a common
sense knowledge,

00:16:34.656 --> 00:16:35.850
how that is represented,

00:16:35.851 --> 00:16:40.520
and how that can be
extracted out of it?

00:16:40.521 --> 00:16:42.220
Another threading
which is parallely

00:16:42.221 --> 00:16:45.270
happening was,
generating images.

00:16:45.271 --> 00:16:47.115
One way to look at generation

00:16:47.116 --> 00:16:49.700
is a goal where you know that,

00:16:49.701 --> 00:16:50.930
okay, you're able
to generate, if

00:16:50.931 --> 00:16:52.305
you're understanding is good.

00:16:52.306 --> 00:16:55.245
Well, that's one way to
look at the generation.

00:16:55.246 --> 00:16:59.485
So, in this case
the popular gaps,

00:16:59.486 --> 00:17:02.110
one with that evades us

00:17:02.111 --> 00:17:03.410
are generated with the help of

00:17:03.411 --> 00:17:05.415
a degenerative model
from the scratch.

00:17:05.416 --> 00:17:08.085
And you have
a discriminative network

00:17:08.086 --> 00:17:10.380
which is basically
asking the question,

00:17:10.381 --> 00:17:12.320
"Can I separate a fake or

00:17:12.321 --> 00:17:16.360
an artificially generated image
from the natural image?"

00:17:16.361 --> 00:17:18.745
There are two modules.

00:17:18.746 --> 00:17:21.310
The first module is
generating an image,

00:17:21.311 --> 00:17:24.240
it's a trainable module,
which is generating an image.

00:17:24.241 --> 00:17:27.070
The second one is
trying to separate an

00:17:27.071 --> 00:17:28.760
artificially generated one from

00:17:28.761 --> 00:17:30.620
the naturally generated one.

00:17:30.621 --> 00:17:34.510
Now, so these two modules

00:17:34.511 --> 00:17:36.420
compete each other
and they play a game

00:17:36.421 --> 00:17:39.135
and learn to perform better over

00:17:39.136 --> 00:17:41.950
time and learn to
generate images,

00:17:41.951 --> 00:17:44.305
which are highly natural.

00:17:44.306 --> 00:17:46.845
So, these are
few examples of images

00:17:46.846 --> 00:17:49.560
that are generated
automatically.

00:17:49.561 --> 00:17:52.210
These are not
realistic bedrooms.

00:17:52.211 --> 00:17:55.910
These are not real people
whose faces are being shown,

00:17:55.911 --> 00:17:58.660
but they are generated

00:17:58.661 --> 00:18:01.545
automatically with
a generative model.

00:18:01.546 --> 00:18:04.995
This generation also provides us

00:18:04.996 --> 00:18:07.840
mechanism for represent
building networks,

00:18:07.841 --> 00:18:10.430
which has plenty
other side roles

00:18:10.431 --> 00:18:14.495
in terms of building better
representations, et cetera.

00:18:14.496 --> 00:18:17.405
So, this is another example
of such generation

00:18:17.406 --> 00:18:19.085
where if you have a set video,

00:18:19.086 --> 00:18:21.085
can you predict the next frame?

00:18:21.086 --> 00:18:24.500
Is it possible for you to
predict the next frame and then

00:18:24.501 --> 00:18:27.545
see how that frame is like?

00:18:27.546 --> 00:18:29.480
This tells us more that our

00:18:29.481 --> 00:18:31.625
understanding of where
the objects will move,

00:18:31.626 --> 00:18:32.830
where what'll happen in the next

00:18:32.831 --> 00:18:34.070
frame and how that will have to

00:18:34.071 --> 00:18:37.985
be visualized in
the pixel space itself.

00:18:37.986 --> 00:18:40.820
And also, like if you are

00:18:40.821 --> 00:18:43.705
given more specific information,

00:18:43.706 --> 00:18:45.310
can you generate images,

00:18:45.311 --> 00:18:49.430
which are more closer
to the description or

00:18:49.431 --> 00:18:54.585
the information that is
given as a prior information.

00:18:54.586 --> 00:18:57.280
So, there are examples
like generating

00:18:57.281 --> 00:18:59.380
birds which are
of specific type,

00:18:59.381 --> 00:19:01.045
which are sitting
in specific form,

00:19:01.046 --> 00:19:03.185
et cetera, are
generated in this way.

00:19:03.186 --> 00:19:04.565
So, we have control over

00:19:04.566 --> 00:19:08.035
the generation process
in a much larger pack.

00:19:08.036 --> 00:19:12.880
And finally, in the space
of image analysis,

00:19:12.881 --> 00:19:15.400
the low-level vision or
a number of task has been

00:19:15.401 --> 00:19:18.475
formulated as an image
2 image transformation.

00:19:18.476 --> 00:19:21.110
And in this space, it
has also changed a lot

00:19:21.111 --> 00:19:24.760
in the last year
or last few years.

00:19:24.761 --> 00:19:27.625
To share a few examples,

00:19:27.626 --> 00:19:29.135
how do you generate

00:19:29.136 --> 00:19:32.805
natural-looking images,
like colorization?

00:19:32.806 --> 00:19:36.070
Or, how do you convert
an image into a new style or

00:19:36.071 --> 00:19:39.610
a new painting style
automatically?

00:19:39.611 --> 00:19:43.485
Or, how do you generate
a high resolution image?

00:19:43.486 --> 00:19:46.425
Or, how do you create a 3D model

00:19:46.426 --> 00:19:49.810
from the 2D images directly.

00:19:49.811 --> 00:19:51.845
In many of these cases,

00:19:51.846 --> 00:19:54.850
our prior understanding of this,

00:19:54.851 --> 00:19:57.480
how this has to be done is

00:19:57.481 --> 00:19:59.555
encoded into the neural network

00:19:59.556 --> 00:20:02.860
with the help of huge amount
of data that it see.

00:20:02.861 --> 00:20:05.635
So, let me summarize.

00:20:05.636 --> 00:20:08.285
So, the computer vision
has been seeing

00:20:08.286 --> 00:20:11.580
a steady progress towards

00:20:11.581 --> 00:20:14.510
looking at harder
and harder tasks.

00:20:14.511 --> 00:20:17.920
Moving from two class
to tons of classes,

00:20:17.921 --> 00:20:21.375
to thousands of classes to
ten thousands of classes,

00:20:21.376 --> 00:20:23.205
and also providing structure

00:20:23.206 --> 00:20:25.145
to the output space in terms of

00:20:25.146 --> 00:20:29.850
the fine grain representations
of the classes.

00:20:29.851 --> 00:20:32.000
In the detection case like face,

00:20:32.001 --> 00:20:33.210
the classical example of face

00:20:33.211 --> 00:20:34.400
detection that we all use on

00:20:34.401 --> 00:20:35.410
our mobile phones to

00:20:35.411 --> 00:20:38.140
deformable objects and
more complex objects,

00:20:38.141 --> 00:20:40.740
pixel-level segmentations,
final understanding of

00:20:40.741 --> 00:20:44.160
the image into
multiple labels and regions.

00:20:44.161 --> 00:20:48.110
Detections, bringing
semantics by

00:20:48.111 --> 00:20:50.705
connecting to the language,

00:20:50.706 --> 00:20:53.920
nouns, verbs, and
various semantic structures,

00:20:53.921 --> 00:20:55.580
interacting with visual data.

00:20:55.581 --> 00:20:59.965
Image-to-Image translation,
capability to synthesize.

00:20:59.966 --> 00:21:04.480
This is what the community is
moving in the recent years.

00:21:04.481 --> 00:21:07.155
That's all. Thank you.

